## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The user's unique identifier")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Create a new user in the system.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateUser(
        [Description("User's full name")] string name,
        [Description("User's email address")] string email,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, email });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/users", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: logs must go to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates directly with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`:

1. **Constructor injection** — Non-static tool classes (like `ApiTools` above) are resolved from the DI container. Services like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, or your own interfaces are injected via primary constructor or standard constructors.

2. **Method parameter injection** — For static tool classes, DI services can appear as method parameters. The SDK resolves them automatically and **excludes them from the tool's JSON schema** — the LLM never sees them.

3. **Framework-injected types** — `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and `RequestContext<T>` are always injected automatically regardless of class style.

4. **Schema rule** — Only parameters with `[Description]` appear in the tool schema exposed to the LLM. DI services and framework types are invisible to callers.

**TL;DR:** Register services in `Program.cs` normally (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.), then accept them in your tool class constructor or method parameters — the SDK handles the rest.
